# NCCL tuning package for the container release (#599)
# =====================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND IS NOT SOURCED BY DEFAULT
# ---------------------------------------------------
# This rig serves on barlink, the fork's own collective library. Container
# users may well run NCCL instead, so #599 parks all NCCL performance work
# until immediately before the Docker release rather than tuning NCCL
# continuously against a transport nobody here runs. The rule until then:
# window arms for performance features run on the barlink operating point,
# and NCCL appears at most as an UNCHANGED control arm, never as a tuning
# target (memory/barlink-standard.md).
#
# So this is a package the release FLIPS ON deliberately, one line at a time,
# each with the evidence that justifies it. It is not a set of defaults, and
# sourcing it wholesale is not the intended use.
#
# HOW TO READ THE LABELS
# ----------------------
#   [AUTO]      Already set by the fork at runtime. Listed so you know it is
#               handled and do NOT set it by hand; a manual value can lose to
#               or fight with the automatic one.
#   [UNIVERSAL] Holds on any hardware. Safe to hand a stranger.
#   [RIG]       Measured on THIS rig and conditioned on its weaknesses. This
#               rig has no P2P and no NVLink: all GPU pairs report PHB via
#               nvidia-smi topo -m, one card sits on a x4 link, and NCCL
#               stages through the host here (docs/rig-runbook.md 6.4,
#               3834-3839). The runbook's own instruction at 3838-3839 is
#               "do not gate features on this rig's weaknesses either --
#               other people's hardware has NVLink." A [RIG] line handed to a
#               user with NVLink is a performance regression, not a fix.
#   [UNMEASURED] Shipped or proposed without a measurement behind it. Said
#               out loud so nobody cites it as tuned.
#
# =====================================================================
# 1. VERSION FLOOR -- not tuning, a hard requirement
# =====================================================================
#
# [UNIVERSAL] Multi-rank-per-GPU co-location (--rank-gpu-id with duplicates)
# requires NCCL >= 2.30. Measured: 2.28.9 REJECTS the co-located communicator,
# 2.30.7 builds it and serves (python/sglang/srt/entrypoints/engine.py
# 1604-1610). torch 2.11 ships 2.28.9, so the image force-reinstalls 2.30.7
# (docker/htsglang.Dockerfile, NCCL_VERSION / NCCL_PACKAGE args) and asserts
# the bundled library at build time by grepping the version out of
# libnccl.so.2.
#
# GAP, deliberately not closed here: there is NO runtime version check. A user
# who overrides NCCL_VERSION below 2.30 and asks for co-location gets NCCL's
# own "Duplicate GPU detected", not a message from this fork. Closing it means
# editing the serving path, which #599 prep explicitly does not do. Booked as
# a follow-up in docs/dev/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md.
NCCL_VERSION_FLOOR=2.30

# =====================================================================
# 2. CONTAINER RUNTIME -- the one boot-blocking item, and it is universal
# =====================================================================
#
# [UNIVERSAL] Docker's default /dev/shm is 64 MB and the default IPC namespace
# is private. NCCL aborts at ncclGroupEnd() with "unhandled system error"
# under those defaults. Measured while benchmarking against llama.cpp on this
# box (docs/benchmarks/llamacpp.json:97, field NCCL_DOCKER_REQUIREMENT;
# restated in docs/benchmarks/llm_compare_2026-07.md:255).
#
# This is a property of the container runtime, not of the cards, so it holds
# for every user. It is a `docker run` flag pair, not an env var, and belongs
# on the run line:
#
#     --ipc=host --shm-size=4g
#
# The same source records two env vars alongside it. They are NOT universal
# and are split out below.

# [RIG] From the same measurement, and this is the part that must not travel:
# "no P2P on these GeForce cards". Disabling P2P on hardware that HAS working
# P2P forces host staging and costs bandwidth. Set only after confirming the
# target machine also reports no peer access -- scripts/p2p_readiness/
# capability_matrix.py emits nvidia-smi topo -m plus cudaDeviceCanAccessPeer
# per ordered pair, which is the check to run.
# NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1

# [AUTO] The fork already sets NCCL_NVLS_ENABLE=0 when --rank-gpu-id
# co-locates ranks, and warns when it does (engine.py:1611-1615,1630-1631):
# NVLS (NVLink SHARP) registration is not valid when two ranks share a device,
# and the reason for setting it explicitly rather than relying on NCCL's
# silent fallback is that the fallback is silent. Setting it by hand is only
# needed for the non-co-located GeForce case above.
# NCCL_NVLS_ENABLE=0

# =====================================================================
# 3. SETTINGS THE FORK ALREADY MANAGES -- listed so you leave them alone
# =====================================================================
#
# [AUTO] NCCL_MULTI_RANK_GPU_ENABLE=1 when --rank-gpu-id contains duplicates,
# and only if unset (engine.py:1624-1625).
#
# [AUTO][UNMEASURED] NCCL_MAX_CTAS = max(1, 8 // ranks_per_gpu), set only for
# the co-located case (engine.py:1638-1640). The intent is stated at
# engine.py:1616-1619 -- leave SM headroom so co-located ranks' NCCL kernels
# do not starve each other -- and the code calls itself "heuristically
# capped". There is no measurement behind the constant 8. Ship it as-is; do
# NOT present it as tuned, and do not let a release note imply it was
# measured. Measuring it is a one-boot experiment: see the A/B recipe below.
#
# [AUTO] NCCL_CUMEM_ENABLE follows --enable-symm-mem (engine.py:1358-1359).
# Note that symm-mem OVERRIDES a user-supplied value rather than deferring to
# it.
#
# [AUTO] NCCL_GRAPH_MIXING_SUPPORT=0, cited to nccl-tests issue 333 as helping
# symmetric kernels (engine.py:1368-1372). DEFECT CANDIDATE, found while
# assembling this package and NOT fixed here because it is a serving-path
# change: the surrounding guard talks about enable_symm_mem while the actual
# predicate is dcp_size > 1, so the two do not describe the same condition.
# Booked in docs/dev/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md; it wants its own ticket and its own
# boot, not a drive-by edit in a release-prep branch.
#
# [AUTO] NCCL_ALGO=allreduce:tree when --enable-deterministic-inference is on
# with tp_size > 1 on CUDA (server_args.py:15992). Determinism, not
# performance -- it also disables the custom all-reduce.

# =====================================================================
# 4. DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED -- with the reason, so nobody re-adds them
# =====================================================================
#
# An empty package would be indistinguishable from an unfinished one, so the
# three things a reader would expect to find here are named with the evidence
# that removed them.
#
# (a) THE "EVERY=32 ABORT GATE" IS NOT AN NCCL SETTING. It gates barlink's
#     BAR1 abort-status word --- SGLANG_BARLINK_BAR1_ABORT_CHECK_EVERY,
#     defined at barlink_abort_gate.py:78 and implemented at :189-197 --- and
#     has no NCCL analogue whatsoever. Shipping it in an NCCL package would
#     ship a knob that does nothing. It is also DEAD on its own terms: it was
#     the #600 interim mitigation for the in-line device read, and once #517's
#     watchdog poll landed, throttling to every 32nd collective was measured
#     to buy nothing (38.89 vs 38.88 ms/round, floors 0.8 % / 0.26 % --
#     docs/dev/window10/RESULTS.md:88-90, removal recommended at :155-161).
#     It has already been removed from the production launch script. Note
#     also that 32 was never derived: the file's registered default is 1, and
#     the 32 existed only as an operator-set value.
#
# (b) THE #244 THRESHOLDS ARE UCX CROSSOVERS, NOT NCCL ALGORITHM THRESHOLDS.
#     SGLANG_BARLINK_UCX_RING_KIB=24 (environ.py:766-771) and the all_gather
#     sibling at 32 KiB (environ.py:774-781) are crossovers in UCX's
#     rendezvous behaviour, measured cross-rig with world 4 over ONE 40G RoCE
#     link on CPU tensors (scripts/r3val/link_collective_cost.py:12-25). They
#     do not translate to NCCL_ALGO or NCCL_PROTO, and the fork's own audit
#     already classifies that geometry as one THIS RIG CANNOT REACH
#     (docs/dev/AUDIT_505_silent_wrongness.md:1270). Worth knowing what the
#     work produced -- a 385.9 -> 198.0 us improvement at 80 KiB, byte-exact
#     (docs/dev/INTEGRATION_R3_VALIDATION.md:4658-4660) -- and worth not
#     mistaking for an NCCL result.
#
# (c) THE IB / RoCE RECIPES IN docs/references/** ARE UPSTREAM-INHERITED.
#     NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX, NCCL_IB_HCA, NCCL_IB_TC=136, NCCL_IB_SL=5,
#     NCCL_IB_QPS_PER_CONNECTION, NCCL_NET_PLUGIN=none, NCCL_MIN_NCHANNELS=4
#     appear in DeepSeek-V3.2 multi-node manifests carried in from upstream
#     (docs/references/multi_node_deployment/lws_pd/lws_pd_deploy.md:107-118
#     and repeats). No fork measurement backs any of them. Passing them off as
#     rig findings would be exactly the #251 defect -- someone else's claim
#     re-shipped as ours.
#
#     Separately, and worth flagging for anyone who tries NCCL over this
#     rig's RoCE link at all: NCCL's verbs path DIES on this fabric with
#     IBV_WC_REM_INV_REQ_ERR(9) on the first 5120-byte proxy tensor while UCX
#     drives the same two HCAs fine, so the cross-rig scripts force
#     NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 and fall back to sockets at 2.0 GB/s and 142 us
#     one-way (scripts/pp/pp_crossrig_rank.sh:66-92). That is diagnosed as a
#     foreign bug in NCCL's RoCE setup, not one of ours, and it is [RIG].
#
# (d) NCCL_BUFFSIZE IS NOT SETTABLE FROM WHERE WE WOULD WANT IT.
#     libnccl allocates transport buffers inside ncclCommInitRank from
#     NCCL_BUFFSIZE x channels x peers plus algorithm staging, "none of which
#     this process chooses" (python/sglang/srt/mem_ledger/engine.py:1211-1213).
#     #599 lists a TERM_NCCL_BUFFERS measurement on a real NCCL transport boot
#     as open work; it needs the container, and it is a MEASUREMENT, not a
#     setting to ship. Booked in RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md.

# =====================================================================
# 5. ONE-BOOT A/B RECIPE, for whoever does get a container window
# =====================================================================
#
# Two things above are worth a real measurement and neither has one:
# NCCL_MAX_CTAS (the unmeasured 8) and the NCCL_BUFFSIZE ingest from #599.
# The method is the rig's standard one, so results compare against everything
# else measured here (memory/ms-pro-runde-als-messlatte.md):
#
#   * Measure ms per ROUND per worker, split COMPUTE vs WAIT. Never clock or
#     power -- those are not a decision basis.
#   * Runs of at least 10 s, warmup discarded, arms back-to-back in one boot.
#   * Run the A-vs-A noise floor FIRST. Two identical arms establish what an
#     apparent difference has to beat; without it a 2 % result means nothing.
#   * Fix the content axis. Throughput on this stack follows the output
#     CONTENT (r = 0.90, memory/spec-bench-inhaltsvarianz.md), so the same
#     prompt set must drive every arm.
#   * Change ONE variable per arm. The #244 write-up names the cost of not
#     doing this: that boot carried two changes and could not attribute them.
#
# Arms for NCCL_MAX_CTAS, on a genuine co-located boot (--rank-gpu-id with a
# duplicate), which is the only configuration where the fork sets it:
#
#   A0/A1  automatic value, twice, back-to-back      -> the noise floor
#   B      NCCL_MAX_CTAS unset (NCCL's own default)  -> is the cap earning it
#   C      NCCL_MAX_CTAS=<2x automatic>              -> direction and slope
#
# If B is inside the A0/A1 floor, the cap is decoration and should be recorded
# as such rather than kept for appearances.
